  "skill": "pharmacology",
  "presentation": "Warfarin interactions",
  "difficulty": "Moderate",
  "stem": "A patient stable on warfarin is started on a course of an enzyme-inhibiting antibiotic (e.g. a macrolide). What is the most likely effect on their anticoagulation?",
  "options": {
   "A": "Warfarin will stop working entirely as an anticoagulant",
   "B": "The antibiotic replaces the need for warfarin",
   "C": "The INR will rise, increasing bleeding risk",
   "D": "The INR will fall, increasing thrombotic clot risk",
   "E": "No change in INR"
  },
  "correct_letter": "C",
  "correct_answer": "C. The INR will rise, increasing bleeding risk",
  "why_correct": "Enzyme-inhibiting drugs (e.g. macrolides, some antifungals, metronidazole) reduce warfarin metabolism, raising warfarin levels and the INR and increasing bleeding risk. The INR should be monitored closely and the warfarin dose adjusted; conversely, enzyme inducers lower the INR.",
  "why_wrong": "A. Warfarin is potentiated, not abolished. B. The antibiotic does not replace anticoagulation. D. Inhibitors raise, not lower, the INR. E. A clinically important change is expected.",
  "pearl": "Enzyme inhibitor + warfarin → INR rises (bleeding risk); inducers → INR falls.",
  "thinking": "Warfarin + enzyme-inhibiting antibiotic ↓ reduced warfarin metabolism ↓ INR rises, monitor closely",
  "exam_trap": "Missing that an enzyme-inhibiting antibiotic raises the INR — the bleeding risk needs closer monitoring.",
  "takeaway": "Enzyme inhibitors raise the INR in warfarin patients, increasing bleeding risk.",
