  "correct_letter": "E",
  "correct_answer": "E. Erythroderma — a dermatological emergency",
  "why_correct": "Erythroderma is generalised erythema affecting more than ~90% of the skin and is a dermatological emergency, risking hypothermia, fluid/protein loss, high-output cardiac failure and infection. It needs admission, supportive care (warmth, fluids), and treatment of the underlying cause (e.g. psoriasis, eczema, drug reaction, lymphoma).",
  "why_wrong": "A. Contact dermatitis is localised. B. Tinea is annular and localised. C. Pityriasis rosea is a self-limiting truncal eruption, not near-total erythema with systemic upset. D. Urticaria is transient weals, not whole-body erythema with systemic upset.",
  "pearl": "Erythema over >90% of skin + systemic upset = erythroderma → admit, supportive care, treat cause.",
