  "correct_answer": "B. Start at a low dose and titrate up slowly",
  "why_correct": "In elderly patients and those with ischaemic heart disease, levothyroxine is started at a low dose (e.g. 25 micrograms) and titrated up slowly with monitoring, because rapid full replacement increases myocardial oxygen demand and can precipitate angina or arrhythmia.",
  "why_wrong": "A. High-dose T3 is hazardous and not standard replacement. C. A full starting dose risks precipitating angina/arrhythmia. D. Hypothyroidism still needs treating — just cautiously. E. Loading doses are inappropriate.",
