  "difficulty": "Moderate",
  "stem": "A 46-year-old man with coarsening facial features, enlarging hands and headaches has a raised serum IGF-1. What is the most appropriate test to confirm the diagnosis of acromegaly?",
  "options": {
   "A": "Oral glucose tolerance test with growth hormone measurement",
   "B": "A random serum growth hormone level",
   "C": "Short Synacthen test",
   "D": "Overnight dexamethasone suppression test with serum cortisol measurement",
   "E": "Thyroid function tests"
  },
  "correct_letter": "A",
  "correct_answer": "A. Oral glucose tolerance test with growth hormone measurement",
  "why_correct": "IGF-1 is the screening test; the diagnosis is confirmed with an oral glucose tolerance test measuring growth hormone — in acromegaly, GH fails to suppress with glucose (normally glucose suppresses GH). Pituitary MRI then localises the adenoma.",
  "why_wrong": "B. Random GH is unreliable due to pulsatile secretion. C. Synacthen tests adrenal function. D. Dexamethasone suppression tests for Cushing's. E. TFTs are not confirmatory for acromegaly.",
  "pearl": "Acromegaly: IGF-1 screens, OGTT-GH confirms (GH fails to suppress).",
  "thinking": "Acromegaly features + raised IGF-1 ↓ confirm ↓ OGTT with GH (non-suppression), then pituitary MRI",
