  "correct_answer": "A. Kawasaki disease",
  "why_correct": "Fever for five or more days with at least four of: bilateral non-purulent conjunctivitis, mucositis (strawberry tongue/cracked lips), polymorphous rash, extremity changes (red, swollen, later peeling hands and feet), and cervical lymphadenopathy is Kawasaki disease. Recognising it matters because of the risk of coronary artery aneurysms and the need for timely IVIG.",
  "why_wrong": "B. Scarlet fever has a sandpaper rash and responds to penicillin, without this prolonged fever pattern. C. Measles has cough, coryza, conjunctivitis and Koplik spots. D. Systemic JIA has a salmon-pink rash with daily fevers and arthritis. E. Adenovirus does not cause this full constellation of features.",
  "pearl": "≥5 days fever + 4 of the 5 features = Kawasaki; think coronary arteries.",
