  "correct_answer": "A. Abdominal ultrasound",
  "why_correct": "Intussusception is diagnosed with abdominal ultrasound, which shows the characteristic 'target'/'doughnut' sign. After resuscitation, a stable child is treated with an air (or contrast) enema reduction; surgery is reserved for failed reduction, perforation or peritonitis.",
  "why_wrong": "B. AXR may show signs but is not the definitive diagnostic test. C. Barium meal is not the investigation of choice. D. Colonoscopy is not used. E. CT is not first-line in this infant.",
  "pearl": "Intussusception: ultrasound (target sign) to diagnose; air enema to reduce.",
  "thinking": "Colicky crying + legs up + redcurrant stool + sausage mass ↓ intussusception ↓ ultrasound",
  "exam_trap": "Reaching for X-ray or CT first — ultrasound is the diagnostic test for intussusception.",
