  "why_correct": "Jaundice appearing within the first 24 hours of life is always pathological and needs urgent investigation (including a conjugated/unconjugated split, blood group, Coombs test, FBC and infection screen), because causes such as haemolysis (e.g. rhesus/ABO incompatibility) or sepsis require prompt treatment. Physiological jaundice does not appear until after 24 hours.",
  "why_wrong": "A. Sunlight is not a treatment and delays care. B. A week's delay risks kernicterus from an untreated cause. C. Stopping breastfeeding is not indicated. E. Physiological jaundice appears after 24 hours, not before.",
